/td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.90</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">F</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.20</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Tuberculosis</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Meta-analysis of cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional studies <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="pmed.1000058-Lin1">&#x0005b;93&#x0005d;</xref></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.62</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="nt131"><label>a</label><p>We used ACS CPS-II as the source of RRs because the Smoking Impact Ratio (SIR), which was used as the exposure metric for tobacco smoking in the main analysis, is calculated using ACS CPS-II cohort and because the study provided separate RRs for different cancers and cardiovascular diseases by age. The CPS-II RRs were also adjusted for multiple potential confounders.</p></fn><fn id="nt132"><label>b</label><p>For these disease outcomes, RRs in the source were reported for all ages combined. We used median age at event and the age pattern of excess risk from IHD to estimate RRs for each age category.</p></fn><fn id="nt133"><label>c</label><p>This category includes lower respiratory tract infections and asthma.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pmed-1000058-t007" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pmed.1000058.t007</object-id><label>Table 7</label><caption><title>Sources and magnitudes of relative risks for the effects of metabolic risk factors on disease-specific mortality.</title></caption><graphic id="pmed-1000058-t007-7" xlink:href="pmed.1000058.t007"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pmed-1000058-t007-7"><colgroup span="1"><c